patches.behavior:
Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems("loop");
const picks = ctx.inputItems<number>("index");
const out = [];
for (const raw of picks) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(raw)) continue;
  const i = Math.floor(raw);
  if (i < 0 || i >= items.length) continue;   // skip; never count from the end
  out.push(items[i]);
}
ctx.output("output", out);                    // whole-loop outputs take item arrays
ctx.output("outputIndex", out.map((_, k) => k));
```

- Loops: `loop` and `index` are whole-loop inputs, so the patch evaluates once per frame. The output length is the number of valid indices, not the longest input. A plain index arrives as a one-item array and gives a one-item loop, which downstream patches and layers treat like a single value.
- Types: `typeParam` sets the item type of `loop` and `output`. There's no pulse type: select a looped Tap with the boolean type, and its one-frame true reads as a pulse downstream.
- Frame 0: outputs on frame 0.
- Edge cases: `index` is a number port with `step: 1`, not an `index` port, because core coerces `index` values below 0 to 0. That way −1 (for example from Option Equals when nothing matches) is skipped instead of selecting the first item. Non-integer indices round down (2.7 → 2; −0.5 → −1, skipped). NaN and ±Infinity are skipped. An empty `loop` gives empty outputs. Duplicate indices repeat items ([0, 0] gives the first item twice).
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: with a scalar index, Origami's Index output echoes that index, while with an index loop it outputs 0…m−1. Sonobe always outputs 0…m−1 so both cases follow one rule.

State per instance: `{ option: 0, prev: [] }`. The patch reduces a whole loop, so it keeps one state, not one per loop index.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems<boolean>("select");
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  if (items[i] && !state.prev[i]) state.option = i;   // ascending, so the highest firing index wins
}
state.prev = items.slice();
const n = items.length;
ctx.output("option", n === 0 ? 0 : Math.min(state.option, n - 1));
```

- Pulses: `select` is a whole-loop pulse input. An item fires on an upstream pulse or on a false→true edge of a connected boolean state, so an item held on selects once and later taps on other items still win. `ctx.pulsed` has no per-item form for whole-loop ports, so the sketch detects edges against last frame's items. The only difference from true per-item pulse detection: a pulse repeated on the same item on the next frame counts once, which changes the result only when a lower index also fires on that second frame.
- Precedence: when several items fire in one frame, the highest index wins.
- Frame 0: Option starts at 0. `prev` starts empty (all off), so an item that's on during frame 0 selects on frame 0.
- Loops: Option is a plain index, so the patch ends the loop. When the loop shrinks, the stored option is kept but the output is capped at count − 1, following the option-patch clamping rule; if the loop grows back, the stored option returns. An empty loop outputs 0. A plain pulse that isn't a loop arrives as a one-item array, so Option stays 0.
- Restart and dispose: Option resets to 0 and `prev` clears. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami leaves the input unlabeled. Its starting value and same-frame tie-breaking aren't documented; Sonobe starts at 0 and lets the highest index win.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems<boolean>("loop");
const groups = ctx.inputItems<number>("grouping");
if (groups.every((g) => !(g >= 0))) {
  ctx.output("output", items.some(Boolean));   // plain boolean
  return;
}
// grouped: see Grouping below
```

- Loops: `loop` and `grouping` are whole-loop inputs, so the patch evaluates once per frame. Without grouping, the output is a plain boolean, which ends the loop.
- Grouping: when every `grouping` item is negative or not finite (the default −1), `output` is a plain boolean. Otherwise `output` is a loop of booleans whose length is the highest group id + 1 (non-integer ids round down). Item i belongs to group `grouping[i % grouping.length]`, so a shorter Grouping loop wraps. Items with a negative or non-finite id are ignored. Groups with no items answer false. Group ids of 10,000 or more are ignored with a warning once per restart, matching the engine's loop limit.
  ```
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    const g = Math.floor(groups[i % groups.length]);
    if (!(g >= 0 && g < 10000)) continue;
    while (result.length <= g) result.push(false);
    if (items[i]) result[g] = true;
  }
  ctx.output("output", result);   // emitted as a loop
  ```
- Pulses: stateless, so with a loop of Tap pulses the output is true exactly on frames where any item pulses. It's a boolean state, not a pulse: if two different items pulse on consecutive frames, the output stays true for both frames and a downstream pulse input sees one rising edge.
- Frame 0: evaluates on frame 0.
- Edge cases: an empty loop gives false (or an empty loop when grouped). A plain boolean arrives as a one-item array and passes through unchanged.
- Muted: Output is false, the boolean zero value. The loop never passes into Output, so nothing downstream starts looping.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami documents Grouping only as an advanced index input defaulting to −1. Sonobe reads each Grouping item as the group id of the matching Loop item, which recovers one answer per instance from a looped component's flattened outputs.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems<boolean>("loop");
const groups = ctx.inputItems<number>("grouping");
if (groups.every((g) => !(g >= 0))) {
  ctx.output("output", items.every(Boolean));  // plain boolean; true for an empty loop
  return;
}
// grouped: see Grouping below
```

- Loops: `loop` and `grouping` are whole-loop inputs, so the patch evaluates once per frame. Without grouping, the output is a plain boolean, which ends the loop.
- Grouping: when every `grouping` item is negative or not finite (the default −1), `output` is a plain boolean. Otherwise `output` is a loop of booleans whose length is the highest group id + 1 (non-integer ids round down). Item i belongs to group `grouping[i % grouping.length]`, so a shorter Grouping loop wraps. Items with a negative or non-finite id are ignored. Groups with no items answer true, the same vacuous truth as an empty loop. Group ids of 10,000 or more are ignored with a warning once per restart, matching the engine's loop limit.
  ```
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    const g = Math.floor(groups[i % groups.length]);
    if (!(g >= 0 && g < 10000)) continue;
    while (result.length <= g) result.push(true);
    if (!items[i]) result[g] = false;
  }
  ctx.output("output", result);   // emitted as a loop
  ```
- Pulses: stateless. With a loop of pulses the output is true only on a frame where every item pulses at once.
- Frame 0: evaluates on frame 0.
- Edge cases: an empty loop gives true (or an empty loop when grouped). A plain boolean arrives as a one-item array and passes through unchanged.
- Muted: Output is false, the boolean zero value (not the empty loop's true). The loop never passes into Output, so nothing downstream starts looping.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami has no All patch. Rebuild one there with Not on each item, Any, then Not; import that chain as is.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems("loop");
const counts = ctx.inputItems<number>("include");
const out = [];
if (items.length > 0 && counts.length > 0) {
  const pairs = Math.max(items.length, counts.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < pairs; i++) {
    const c = counts[i % counts.length];
    const times = c > 0 ? Math.floor(Math.min(c, 10000)) : 0;   // NaN, 0, and negatives drop
    for (let k = 0; k < times; k++) {
      if (out.length === 10000) { warnOnce("Loop Filter: result capped at 10,000 items."); break; }
      out.push(items[i % items.length]);
    }
  }
}
ctx.output("output", out);                   // whole-loop outputs take item arrays
ctx.output("index", out.map((_, k) => k));
```

- Loops: `loop` and `include` are whole-loop inputs, so the patch evaluates once per frame. The output length follows the counts, not the longest input: it's the sum of the rounded-down counts, capped at 10,000. The two inputs pair item by item, and the shorter one wraps (the standard loop rule). That's how a single item repeats, or a single count applies to every item.
- Types: `typeParam` sets the item type of `loop` and `output`. Include is a number port: on/off values arrive as 1 and 0, and a pulse arrives as 1 on the frame it fires, so a looped Tap in Include gives the tapped item for exactly that frame.
- Frame 0: outputs on frame 0.
- Edge cases: an empty Loop or an empty Include gives empty outputs. Fractional counts round down (2.9 → 2); NaN and counts ≤ 0 drop the item; +Infinity counts as 10,000. `warnOnce` logs through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)` at most once per restart.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami calls the ports Input and Include, then Loop and Index. When the inputs have different lengths, Sonobe wraps the shorter one; Origami doesn't document that case.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const items = ctx.inputItems<number>("loop");
const inclusive = ctx.input<boolean>("includeCurrent");
const totals = [];
let acc = 0;
for (const raw of items) {
  const v = Number.isFinite(raw) ? raw : 0;   // warnOnce when not finite
  if (inclusive) acc += v;
  totals.push(Number.isFinite(acc) ? acc : 0);
  if (!inclusive) acc += v;
}
ctx.output("total", totals);                 // whole-loop output takes the item array
```

- Loops: `loop` is a whole-loop input and `total` is a whole-loop output of the same length, so the patch evaluates once per frame. `includeCurrent` isn't loopable: if a loop arrives there, `ctx.input` reads item 0.
- Frame 0: outputs on frame 0.
- Edge cases: an empty loop gives an empty loop. A plain number gives [0], or [value] with Include Current. Non-finite items count as 0, and a total that overflows to ±Infinity outputs 0 for that item; both warn once per restart through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`. Sums run left to right, so floating-point rounding matches a hand-written loop.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami leaves the output unlabeled; Sonobe calls it Total. Include Current is new, and off matches Origami.

Stateless.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
const finite = (n, fallback) => (Number.isFinite(n) ? n : fallback);
const cols = Math.max(1, Math.floor(finite(ctx.input("columns"), 1)));
const spacing = finite(ctx.input("spacing"), 0);
const itemWidth = Math.max(0, (finite(ctx.input("width"), 0) - spacing * (cols - 1)) / cols);
const itemHeight = Math.max(0, finite(ctx.input("itemHeight"), 0));
const i = ctx.input<number>("index");          // index port: already a whole number ≥ 0
const row = Math.floor(i / cols);
const col = i - row * cols;
const [ox, oy] = ctx.input<number[]>("origin");
ctx.output("position", [ox + col * (itemWidth + spacing), oy + row * (itemHeight + spacing)]);
ctx.output("size", [itemWidth, itemHeight]);
```

- Loops: no whole-loop ports, so the patch evaluates once per loop index. A loop on Index gives loops of positions and sizes of the same length; scalars broadcast; an empty Index loop gives empty outputs. An item's place comes from the Index value, not from the loop position, so a reordered index loop (from Loop Select) moves items.
- Coordinates: `position` is each item's top-left corner in the target layer's parent space (CONVENTIONS.md §18.5.1). With the default Anchor [0, 0] it lines up exactly; for another anchor, add Anchor × Size.
- Frame 0: outputs on frame 0.
- Edge cases: Columns below 1 or not finite count as 1, so there's no division by zero; decimal Columns round down. When Spacing is too wide for Width, item width clamps to 0 and positions still advance by Spacing. Negative Item Height clamps to 0; negative Spacing overlaps items. Index values below 0 arrive as 0 through `index` coercion. Non-finite Width, Item Height, or Spacing count as 0, and non-finite Origin components are already 0 after vector coercion.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami documents that items divide Width evenly but not how Padding affects item width. Sonobe subtracts the gaps first, so items plus gaps exactly fill Width. Importers convert Origami's center-origin positions to top-left.
